---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `print("Hello, Lua!")`;
const Figure1Out = `Hello, Lua!`;

const Figure2 = `-- Lua ignores this line.

--[[
  And every line
  inside this block.
]]

print("Only this line runs.")`;
const Figure2Out = `Only this line runs.`;

const Figure3 = `print("Ada", 42, true)
print("Ada" .. " " .. "42")`;
const Figure3Out = `Ada\t42\ttrue
Ada 42`;

const RunIt = `lua main.lua`;

const Task1 = `print("SKYNEST TERMINAL")`;
const Task1Out = `SKYNEST TERMINAL`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua - Skynest terminal readout

print("SKYNEST TERMINAL")
print("----------------")`;
const Task2Out = `SKYNEST TERMINAL
----------------`;

const Task3 = `-- main.lua - Skynest terminal readout

print("SKYNEST TERMINAL")
print("----------------")
print("Status:", "ONLINE")
print("Reactor:", 98, "percent")
print("Crew:", 4)`;

const Checkpoint = `SKYNEST TERMINAL
----------------
Status:\tONLINE
Reactor:\t98\tpercent
Crew:\t4`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.1: Meet Lua</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            Lua is <b class="text-sn-theme">small on purpose</b>. The whole
            interpreter is about 32,000 lines of C and compiles down to a few
            hundred kilobytes. That number is not trivia &mdash; it is the
            reason you keep running into Lua. It is cheap enough to drop{" "}
            <i>inside</i> another program, so that is where it usually lives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Places you have probably already used Lua without knowing:{" "}
            <b>Roblox</b> (its Luau language is a Lua dialect), <b>World of
            Warcraft</b> addons, the <b>Neovim</b> text editor, and{" "}
            <b>LÖVE</b>, the game framework you will use in Unit 2.
          </p>

          <p>
            The name is not an acronym. <b class="text-sn-theme">Lua means
            "moon" in Portuguese</b> &mdash; it was created at PUC-Rio in
            Brazil, and it is pronounced <i>LOO-ah</i>.
          </p>

          <p>
            One consequence of being small: you can actually finish learning it.
            That is not true of most languages, and it is most of why this
            course exists.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Saying something</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="print"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            writes a line to the terminal. It is the whole of your output
            toolkit for now.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            You run a Lua file by handing it to the interpreter. From a terminal
            in the same folder as your file:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={RunIt}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="sh"
            title="Terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <h3 class="mt-5">Notes to yourself</h3>

          <p>
            Two dashes start a comment. Lua reads to the end of the line and
            ignores all of it. For several lines at once, use{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="--[[ ]]"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <h3 class="mt-5">Printing more than one thing</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="print"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            takes as many values as you give it, of any type, separated by
            commas. Watch the spacing carefully &mdash; this catches people.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure3Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Commas insert a tab, not a space.</b> If
            you want exact control over the spacing, join the pieces yourself
            with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code=".."
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, which is Lua's way of gluing two strings together. Activity 1.3
            is all about that operator.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Make a folder for this course, and inside it create a file named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">main.lua</b>. You will run it after every
            task &mdash; getting into that habit now is worth more than
            anything else on this page.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Get something on the screen</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Print the line <b>SKYNEST TERMINAL</b>.
            </li>
            <li>
              Run it with{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="lua main.lua"
                lang={"sh"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              before you write anything else.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <Code
            code={Task1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Label your file and add a rule</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add a comment at the top saying what the file is.</li>
            <li>Print a line of dashes underneath the title.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "1-2, 4" }}
          />

          <Code
            code={Task2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The comment produced nothing, which is the entire point of it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Report the ship's status</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Print three more lines, each passing <b>several values</b> to one{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="print"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              call, separated by commas.
            </li>
            <li>
              Mix the types on purpose &mdash; text on some, numbers on others.
            </li>
            <li>
              Look at where the gaps land in the output. Those are the tabs.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "5-7" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Rewrite the <b>Status</b> line so the output reads{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="Status: ONLINE"
                lang={"txt"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              with a single space instead of a tab. You will need{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code=".."
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and one{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="print"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              call.
            </li>
            <li>
              Comment out the <b>Crew</b> line without deleting it, and confirm
              it stops appearing.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <p>Running your finished file should produce this.</p>

          <Code
            code={Checkpoint}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            If you got an error instead, read the line number it gives you and
            check that every string has both of its quotes. Lua's error messages
            are short, and they are usually pointing at the right line.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a class="btn btn-outline-light" href="/courses/lua-programming/">
            Course Index
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-2">
            Next: Variables, local, and nil <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
